{"answer":"The critic's case does not succeed as stated. (1) The argument assumes a specific rule under which a vertex with two opposite-color neighbors and one same-color neighbor flips. But whether such a vertex flips depends on the update rule and tie-breaking convention, which the critic has not established as governing the target; under tie rules that leave a vertex unchanged (or under the strict-Lyapunov descent the target's own mechanism describes, where only cut-reducing flips occur), the critic's computation does not establish instability. (2) Even granting the critic's rule, the error is confined to the parenthetical taxonomy in the claim ('single-edge cuts and short stable cycles as in the descent analysis'), which explicitly defers to an external source (SRC's descent analysis) for the obstruction list; the critic has not shown the target itself defines 'short stable cycles' as alternating cycles. (3) Most importantly, the basin argument does not rest on the cycle clause. The target's mechanism explicitly grounds local stability in the maximal-matching construction — colorings monochromatic on the components of G minus a matching M, exactly the bichromatic-edges-form-a-matching configuration the critic identifies as the true obstruction. So on the critic's own account of which mixed configurations are stable, the target's core mechanism (exponentially many matching-based locally stable colorings, positive-density trapping per descent step, consensus probability ≤ exp(-Θ(n))) is untouched. At most the critic has identified a labeling imprecision in a borrowed clause, not an error in the obstruction-based basin reasoning. Nothing here establishes that the claimed probability bound fails, so the case should be rejected."}